{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>And I will bless those who bless you, and whoever curses you I will curse.</b> This indicates that the men of Ur of the Chaldees despised him and cursed him, and therefore He told him to go to the land that He would show him, and there He would bless those who blessed him and curse those who cursed him, and if an individual curses him he will be cursed — thus the Ramban explained. And he said further that the Torah abbreviated the dispute that took place between him and the Chaldees, just as it abbreviated the matter of the generation of Enosh, end quote." "<b>And it is possible</b> to interpret 'whoever curses you' as referring to King Nimrod, for when the king is cursed the people are cursed. And it further appears likely that the men of Ur of the Chaldees, being idolaters of many distinct species, had some who worshipped one species of idolatry such as Molech, and their sages would proclaim throughout their nation that whoever gives of his seed to Molech will prosper in his remaining offspring and become a great nation; and there were those who worshipped another species and believed that through it they would prosper in their wealth, and there were those who worshipped another species and believed that through it their name would grow great and become renowned in the world, and there were those who worshipped another species and believed that through it the worshipper would himself become a source of blessing, to obtain every desire of his, so that all who drew near him would be blessed by his blessing. Therefore the Holy One, blessed be He, mentioned to him corresponding to all these species: 'ואעשך לגוי גדול ואברכך ואגדלה שמך והיה ברכה' — and I will make of you a great nation, and I will bless you, and I will make your name great, and you shall be a blessing — because all the forces, which are the species of idolatry, have no power to add or diminish except according to what is assigned to them in their commission, and all the more so they have no power to do one thing and its opposite, for even the planet Mars, though it stand at a man's right hand and be among his helpers, has no power to help or benefit except in destruction and in ways of harm, not in ways of goodness; and likewise the rest of the forces, whether those that do good or those that do harm, have no power to benefit except according to what is assigned to them alone — whether for good or for evil, they have no power to do its opposite. Therefore He added here: ואברכה מברכיך ומקללך אאר — for this is a thing and its opposite; no force among all the forces has this power, but only the primordial supreme Force — the portion of Jacob, the Fashioner of all — from whom comes both evil and good, in the manner that the prophet said (Isaiah 45:7): ״אני ה׳ עושה כל אלה״." "<b>And in the Midrash:</b> 'ואברכה מברכיך' — the priests bless Israel, as it says (Numbers 6:27): ״ושמו את שמי על בני ישראל ואני אברכם״. The Holy One, blessed be He, said: I bless that tribe. The Holy One, blessed be He, said: In this world the tribe of Levi blesses you, but in the time to come I, in My glory, will bless you; as it is written (Jeremiah 31:23): ״יברכך ה׳ נוה צדק הר הקדשם״." "<b>And all the families of the earth shall bless themselves by you.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): in contrast to the fact that everyone curses you now, you are destined that all the nations shall be blessed through your blessings." "<b>And by way of</b> the Midrash: 'ונברכו בך' — the rains and the dews, in your merit. 'ונברכו בך' — Jacob blessed Pharaoh; Joseph interpreted the dream for him; Daniel interpreted for Nebuchadnezzar — when they enter into trouble they ask us and we reveal to them; as it is written: 'ונברכו בך' — end quote, from Bereshit Rabbah." "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah: ונברכו בך — the blessing from above is the attribute of Avraham, the beginning of the attributes. And to this they alluded in what they expounded on ונברכו בך — that their blessing should pass through you. And the matter is that he is the beginning for receiving the blessing, and from him the blessing extends throughout the entire world. And this is the matter of what is written (Psalms 117:1–2): ״שבחוהו כל האומים״, ״כי גבר עלינו חסדו״ — and this is clear."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {1 "The concluding citation Isaiah 45:7 in full reads 'עושה שלום ובורא רע'; he quotes only the final phrase 'אני ה׳ עושה כל אלה' which appears to be a paraphrastic condensation rather than verbatim text; rendered as he wrote it."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:12:04.978149350Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}